the federal reserve has finally admitted  that inflation wasn't transitory but  they made a mistake and they didn't  quite realize how bad inflation would  get or how persistent it would be  now their response to that 40-year high  in inflation is to tighten financial  conditions they're raising interest  rates aggressively and they're beginning  to conduct quantitative tightening by  selling assets off of their balance  sheet but there's a number of analysts  who believe that these actions are  actually only going to make the problem  worse  in order to understand what this thought  process is you have to realize why the  analysts believe inflation has actually  occurred this group of individuals that  believe that raising interest rates  aren't going to help they have a very  specific view on how we got the high  inflation their general thought process  is that there has been a supply shock  that actually the increase in demand is  not what's driving inflation but more so  the kovid 19 lockdown created all sorts  of supply chain issues geopolitical  uncertainty and conflict like the russia  ukraine war that created supply chain  shocks as well and so those external  shocks to supply chains and  manufacturing around the world that's  driven a very difficult time for  companies when it comes to inventory and  their ability to sell goods and services  now if you believe this thought process  if there's constrained supply and demand  was to stay the same then of course  prices would go higher but i don't  necessarily know if that's the truth and  so let's take a look a little bit deeper  at somebody like paul gambles from mbmg  group which went on cnbc earlier today  and here's what he said supply is very  difficult to manage we're fighting  across a whole bunch of industries a  whole bunch of businesses they're having  very difficult challenges just turning  the taps back on  he then stated that the fed is the first  one to put up their hands and say  monetary policy can't do anything about  a supply shock and then they go and they  raise interest rates  now of course if this was only a  supply-driven inflation problem raising  interest rates would make no sense but i  don't know if you can actually say it's  only supply driven  of course there's undisciplined monetary  and fiscal policy we stuffed all sorts  of liquidity into every exotic corner of  the financial system we literally mailed  checks directly to individuals and if  you make people wealthier either through  increasing asset prices or by simply  mailing them money they're going to  spend it and so we have to remember the  global economy is a complex machine when  you layer in macro and microeconomics  and then things like geopolitics on top  of it it's an impossible task to not  only predict what's going to happen in  the future but to even understand what's  happened in the past and so naturally  what we're watching here is that people  humans they like to boil it down to  simple evaluations we want to try to  understand one aspect of the economy and  then talk in a black and white way as to  what the solution is  but the inflation that we're  experiencing comes from supply and  demand shocks yes there have been  disruptions in the supply chain yes  there was undisciplined monetary and  fiscal policy this is all nuance should  we destroy demand well we probably  shouldn't destroy all the demand just  specific types should we tighten  financial conditions  absolutely in some situations but it  doesn't mean that we should crash asset  prices across the globe in pursuit of  tighter financial conditions in some  sort of blanket way the world is very  complex it's very nuanced and so it's  going to be difficult for people to talk  about potential solutions because the  solutions would need to be nuanced and  that would mean that we trust central  bankers and people in positions of power  and influence to be surgeons to have the  intricate control and command of the  minutia of an economy but that's not how  it works it's never worked that way and  it never will work that way  central bankers only have blunt weapons  they can raise interest rates or they  can drop them they conduct quantitative  easing or they can conduct quantitative  tightening that's pretty much it  and unfortunately those blunt weapons  you can't pick and choose where the  sensitivities where their reactions in  the economy are and where they aren't  ultimately what we have is a mismatch  the tools at our disposal to handle  things like high inflation aren't  necessarily what we need but they're the  best that we have and that's where you  see central bankers economists and many  others trying to figure out how do we go  and look at the constraints of the tools  available to us and use those to get  inflation down  now the risk is if we get inflation down  and we cause a recession that might not  be much better if you went up to someone  and you asked them would you rather high  inflation or a recession they don't want  either  and so naturally the central bank begins  to talk about a soft landing can we  thread the needle can we get inflation  down but not push ourselves into a  recession i tend to think that that will  be very very difficult for anyone  including the central bankers to do that  would require a very intricate  understanding command and control of the  economy and that's just not the world we  live in  and so  should we be raising interest rates to  get inflation down there's probably a  strong argument that that will destroy  demand to some degree and will help tame  inflation but the question is  is the solution better or worse  for what could be around the corner if  we raise interest rates and get  inflation down but maybe we push the  united states and or the global economy  into recession  was the solution worth it that'll be for  each individual person and family to  decide but ultimately the central  bankers they're faced with a lose-lose  situation and there's a lot of folks  including myself who tend to think that  the free market does a better job at  handling these situations that the free  market will be better at self-correcting  than having the intervention from  central banks or any of our elected  officials  now that's a tough ask is to sit by  while we experience high inflation or we  experience some sort of recessive period  but as we've seen time and time and time  again  intervention in the markets especially  around the cost of capital and or  quantitative easing and tightening  it ends up diluting all sorts of aspects  of the market like asset prices and it  ends up mitigating short-term pain at  the expense of long-term problems  the high inflation we're experiencing  right now is a long-term problem  that is due to the short-term pain  mitigation that was conducted during  2020 and the covid crisis  naturally there is no right answer  there's trade-offs in life and there's  trade-offs in monetary policy and the  management of an economy  it's one of the great debates should you  mitigate short-term pain or should you  go ahead and solve the long-term  problems and allow people to experience  that short-term pain that ultimately  leads to a conversation around  incentives and it's one that you i nor  anyone else debating it will end up  solving  stay alert out there we're going into  some pretty crazy times make sure you're  paying attention and you're learning  because at the end of the day if you're  a young person you're going to see these  situations a couple more times in your  lifetime and having the knowledge and  experience that you'll gain over the  coming months will be invaluable to you  as you begin to navigate markets in the  future
 

